      ],
      "explanation": "Substituting \\(r-1\\) into the formula gives\n\\[\nf(r-1)=\\frac13(r-1)r(r+1).\n\\]\nThe expressions for \\(f(r)\\) and \\(f(r-1)\\) share the factors \\(r\\) and \\(r+1\\). Factoring them out avoids expanding the cubic products:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r)-f(r-1)\n&=\\frac{r(r+1)}3\\bigl[(r+2)-(r-1)\\bigr]\\\\\n&=\\frac{r(r+1)}3\\cdot3\\\\\n&=r(r+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the answer is \\(r(r+1)\\), choice A.",
      "id": "factor-shared-product",
      "sourceRef": "amc/1968_AMC_12_Q05#factor-shared-product",
      "title": "Keep the common factors"
    }
